00:07:51.289 --> 00:08:05.150
Besides the color filter you can use the creative
film luts to push things evem further, so

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:09.940
let's jump into davinci to see how it's done

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:14.500
so the basic theory behind Luts in general
are very limited in terms of information they

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:20.900
hold, they can only store Saturation Hue and
Luma Values, which means you cannot add any

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:32.849
effects like red halation and such
And once you put a lut over the image it's

00:08:32.849 --> 00:08:53.300
like a permanent limitation, because 
you cannot adjust the contrast or change the

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:55.209
colors of the lut sepparately

00:08:55.209 --> 00:09:07.059
that's why i segmented the creative film luts
into Base Lut, Film Contrast, Classic Film

00:09:07.059 --> 00:09:12.279
Color and red halation Effects for those who
work in Davinci Resolve

00:09:12.279 --> 00:09:31.249
For those working in finalcut or premiere
pro separating the contrast from the color

00:09:31.249 --> 00:09:44.620
you can actually have more control than any
classic lut which limits your control over

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:49.449
the image and kinda destroys it

00:09:49.449 --> 00:10:10.400
The ones working in dainci can buy the powergrades
which actually gives full control over the

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:16.009
effects and can further inhance it in the
node panel

00:10:16.009 --> 00:10:20.110
in Davinci resolve Install the luts by making
multiple Powergrade folders, import the files

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:21.329
into each folder separately.

00:10:21.329 --> 00:11:50.889
add 4 new nodes by pressing Alt+S 3 times
and then just choose drag 

00:11:50.889 --> 00:12:29.670
and drop onto each node a different effect.

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:45.980
I usually go first node Base Lut
second node Film Contrast

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:57.870
third node Classic 
Film Color

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:01.670
fourth node Red Halation

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:17.089
In premiere or 

00:13:17.089 --> 00:13:40.569
final cut you can do the same by applying
the luts onto different adjustment layers,

00:13:40.569 --> 00:15:30.230
but unfortunately it doesn't include the halation
effects because, like i said earlier, the

00:15:30.230 --> 00:15:34.179
LUTs contain limited information

00:15:34.179 --> 00:15:39.639
In this pack i prepared over 30 different
contrast gammas and over 40 color schemes,

00:15:39.639 --> 00:15:45.379
which results in more than 1000 possible unique
variations, pretty crazy ey?

00:15:45.379 --> 00:15:49.550
Also snucked in the exact same luts i have
used in the original video for each profile

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:51.689
